10:22:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Water does **not** only evaporate at 100°C. - **Evaporation** can happen at almost any temperature. Even cold water slowly loses molecules from its surface because some molecules have enough energy to escape into the air. - **100°C** is the boiling point of water at normal sea-level air pressure. At that temperature, water forms vapor bubbles throughout the liquid—not just at the surface. The Sun speeds up evaporation by warming the water. Warmer water molecules move faster, so more of them have enough energy to escape from the surface as water vapor. Dry air and wind also help: they carry away the water vapor above the surface, allowing more water to evaporate. That is why clothes can dry in sunlight even though they are nowhere near 100°C.
