10:23:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your teacher may have been simplifying the idea that **adding heat can turn liquid water into gas**, or she may have meant **boiling** specifically. But scientifically, “evaporation only happens at 100°C” is not correct. A water-cycle diagram is actually strong evidence of this: lakes, oceans, soil, and plants evaporate water under ordinary outdoor conditions—often 10–30°C. The ocean is not generally 100°C. At sea level: - Water **boils** at about **100°C**. - Water **evaporates** at temperatures below 100°C too, including room temperature and below freezing in some cases. - Higher temperature, sunlight, wind, and dry air make evaporation faster. A simple real-world test is an uncovered bowl of water indoors. Mark the water level, leave it for several days, and it will fall despite never having been heated anywhere near 100°C.
