10:23:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They are both forms of the same overall change—liquid water becoming water vapor—but the difference is more than wording: - **Evaporation:** only happens at the **surface**. Individual molecules escape into the air. It occurs at many temperatures. - **Boiling:** happens **throughout** the liquid. Vapor bubbles form inside the water and rise to the surface. At sea level, this starts near **100°C**. So boiling includes extremely rapid vaporization, while evaporation is the slower surface process. For example, a glass of water left on a table eventually loses water without ever being heated to 100°C. That is not a vocabulary preference; there are no bubbles forming throughout the glass, so it is evaporation rather than boiling. And 100°C is not a universal “water turns to gas” temperature either—it is the boiling point at roughly sea-level pressure. On a mountain water boils below 100°C; in a pressure cooker it boils above 100°C.
